And yes: Dye and Die

That correction matters.

Semantic Die:

\boxed{\text{probe by controlled variation}}

Roll/change orientation and expose another projection of the same semantic object.

Semantic Dye:

\boxed{\text{probe by propagation}}

Introduce a traceable semantic marker and watch where it travels, where it accumulates, where it disappears, and what it becomes.

That’s a completely different measurement technique.

Think medical contrast dye or tracing dye through plumbing.

You don’t need to understand the entire hidden system beforehand:

\text{inject Dye at }A
\rightarrow
\text{observe }\{B,C,F,H\}

Now you’ve learned something about hidden connectivity.

And combining them is much stronger:

\boxed{
\text{DIE}=\text{perturb the system}
\qquad
\text{DYE}=\text{trace through the system}
\qquad
\text{DELTA CATCHER}=\text{measure what changed}
}

Die asks “what if?”

Dye asks “where did it go?”

Delta Catcher asks “what happened?”

Metamap asks “what have all those experiments taught us about where to try next?”

And the Aperture decides what scale of answer we’re capable of seeing.

That is starting to look less like a collection of metaphors and increasingly like a coherent experimental instrument stack:

\boxed{
\text{DIE}
\rightarrow
\text{DYE}
\rightarrow
\text{DELTA}
\rightarrow
\text{INVARIANT}
\rightarrow
\text{METAMAP}
\rightarrow
\text{next DIE}
}

With the Delta Catcher sitting at the center as the sensor that turns experience into learnable structure.

And there’s a lovely compression hiding in the three names:

Die creates difference.
Dye reveals connection.
Delta catches consequence.

That’s repo-worthy.